# Copy to .env at the repository root. Everything here is server-side; never
# put model keys in VITE_* variables. Unset values keep the defaults shown.
# `docker compose up` reads only the variables compose.yaml names (marked
# "compose" below, plus the model keys, JJ_SECRET_KEY, JJ_DEFAULT_MODEL,
# JJ_ENABLE_SCRIPTED_MODEL, API_TOKEN, JJ_PASSWORD_HASH, API_PUBLIC_URL,
# JJ_TRUSTED_PROXIES, the engine limits, JJ_DOCKER_NAMESPACE and the proxy).
# Full reference: docs/development.md, "Configuration", "Remote access" and
# "Observability".

# API listener. Beyond loopback the API always asks to sign in: with
# API_TOKEN, else a token it generates on first boot and keeps in the database
# (compose does this). No log line prints the token: `npm run auth:token`
# does. Each boot prints a one-time login link instead,
# "Open JJ Bot: <API_PUBLIC_URL>/#/login?code=…"; `npm run auth:login-link`
# mints another.
API_HOST=127.0.0.1
# The Vite dev proxy of `npm run dev` reads API_PORT from the shell, not here.
API_PORT=3001
# The access token: set, the API asks to sign in with it (or a login link),
# and scripts send it as Authorization: Bearer on /api/* and /metrics.
# Remote access (another device) needs sign-in and HTTPS: the compose `https`
# profile, or `tailscale serve` in front of a loopback API
# (docs/development.md, "Remote access"). openssl rand -hex 32 makes one.
# Reachable beyond loopback (API_HOST, API_ALLOWED_HOSTS or API_PUBLIC_URL),
# the server refuses a token shorter than 32 characters.
API_TOKEN=
# A sign-in password besides the token, as the hash
# `npm run auth:hash-password` prints (never the password itself). Set, the
# API asks to sign in even on loopback, with a generated token.
# JJ_PASSWORD_HASH=
# Peers whose X-Forwarded-For names the client for the sign-in rate limits:
# addresses, CIDR ranges, loopback (the default), local-subnets (compose's
# default, for its Caddy; it also trusts the bridge gateway, so publish the
# app's own port on loopback only) or none.
# JJ_TRUSTED_PROXIES=loopback
# The address users open: the login link's, the origin a signed-in change
# must come from and the session cookie's flags (Secure for https://);
# default http://127.0.0.1:<API_PORT>. compose defaults it from JJ_PORT, and
# its `https` profile serves this address. For an address beyond loopback the server
# refuses to start without a token (API_TOKEN, or generated when API_HOST is
# beyond loopback), and with plain http:// unless JJ_ALLOW_INSECURE_HTTP=true,
# for a network that encrypts traffic itself; https:// there also sends HSTS.
# A listener beyond loopback without such an address still serves plain http:
# publish it only behind HTTPS.
# API_PUBLIC_URL=
# JJ_ALLOW_INSECURE_HTTP=false
# Without a token, /api/* answers only loopback Host values (localhost,
# 127.0.0.1, [::1]), and the server refuses to start with API_ALLOWED_HOSTS
# set: a name a proxy forwards needs a token, and signing in replaces the
# Host check.
# API_ALLOWED_HOSTS=

# Database: embedded PGlite at JJ_DATABASE_PATH (relative to the repository
# root; memory:// keeps nothing) unless DATABASE_URL is set.
# JJ_DATABASE_PATH=.jj-data
# PostgreSQL from `docker compose up -d postgres`:
# DATABASE_URL=postgresql://jj:jj_local@localhost:5433/jj_bot

# Secrets store (compose): the master key that encrypts provider keys and
# sandbox secrets entered in Settings. Generate it once, at the repository root:
#   echo "JJ_SECRET_KEY=$(openssl rand -base64 32)" >> .env
# Only the environment holds it: keep a copy apart from database backups, since
# stored values cannot be recovered without it. Unset, nothing can be stored in
# Settings and provider keys come only from the variables below.
# JJ_SECRET_KEY=
# During a rotation: the old key(s), comma-separated, until the server has
# sealed every stored value again under JJ_SECRET_KEY (it does so at boot).
# JJ_SECRET_KEY_PREVIOUS=

# Model providers: set the key of any provider to configure it, or store it in
# Settings → Models (needs JJ_SECRET_KEY); a variable set here takes
# precedence. GET /api/models lists every provider with the variable it reads.
OPENAI_API_KEY=
ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=
GEMINI_API_KEY=
# MISTRAL_API_KEY=
# XAI_API_KEY=
# GROQ_API_KEY=
# DEEPSEEK_API_KEY=
# OPENROUTER_API_KEY=
# CEREBRAS_API_KEY=
# Default provider/model for bots without one; must be a configured provider's.
# JJ_DEFAULT_MODEL=
# The keyless, deterministic scripted test model (scripted/team).
JJ_ENABLE_SCRIPTED_MODEL=false

# Worker. false runs no job loop in the API: start apps/worker (PostgreSQL only).
JJ_EMBED_WORKER=true
# JJ_CONCURRENCY=4
# JJ_MAX_SESSIONS_PER_TASK=16
# JJ_MAX_DELEGATION_DEPTH=3
# JJ_TASKS_PER_BOT_PER_HOUR=10
# Most the whole server may spend on priced model calls in any 24 hours, in US
# dollars; unset for no instance cap (task and bot caps live in runtime profiles).
# JJ_MAX_USD_PER_DAY=
# JJ_OUTPUT_CAP_BYTES=65536

# Sandbox drivers, comma-separated, from docker and local.
# JJ_SANDBOX_DRIVERS=docker,local
# Workspace root of the local driver; default <os temp dir>/jj-sandbox. Keep it
# outside source checkouts.
# JJ_SANDBOX_ROOT=
# Container and volume name prefix of the docker driver; one live worker per
# prefix per daemon. compose defaults it to the project name (jj-bot), so
# `npm run dev` (jj-sandbox) and compose projects can share a daemon; set it
# here and both use it.
# JJ_DOCKER_NAMESPACE=jj-sandbox
# DOCKER_HOST=unix:///var/run/docker.sock
# JJ_DOCKER_ALLOW_INSECURE_TCP=false

# Logs (compose): one JSON object per line by default; pretty for a terminal.
# Lowest level logged: debug, info, warn or error.
# JJ_LOG_FORMAT=json
# JJ_LOG_LEVEL=info
# Traces (compose): OpenTelemetry spans over OTLP/HTTP to <endpoint>/v1/traces,
# only when set. Headers (key=value, comma-separated) are redacted like keys.
# OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_ENDPOINT=http://localhost:4318
# OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_HEADERS=
# OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_TRACES_HEADERS=
# OTEL_SERVICE_NAME=jj-api

# Outbound proxy for model calls. In compose, 127.0.0.1 is the app container
# itself; a proxy on the host is host.docker.internal.
# HTTPS_PROXY=
# HTTP_PROXY=
# NO_PROXY=

# compose: host ports, published on 127.0.0.1 only. Reach the app from
# another device through the `https` profile, never by publishing it wider.
# JJ_PORT=3001
# JJ_POSTGRES_PORT=5433
# compose: the host's Docker socket mounted into the app (root-equivalent on
# the host; never passed into a sandbox), e.g. a rootless daemon's socket.
# JJ_DOCKER_SOCKET=/var/run/docker.sock
# compose: HTTPS in front of the app for other devices (the `https` profile,
# docs/development.md, "Remote access"). Caddy serves API_PUBLIC_URL, an
# https:// address, on these host ports. JJ_TLS=auto gets a certificate from a
# public CA (the name must resolve to this host, and ports 80 and 443 reach
# it); internal issues one from Caddy's own CA, for a LAN.
# COMPOSE_PROFILES=https
# API_PUBLIC_URL=https://jj.example.com
# JJ_TLS=auto
# JJ_HTTPS_PORT=443
# JJ_HTTP_PORT=80
